\u2019s 4 pairs: W5, W9, B2, and potential pair with F3 if I get another F3. Singles: T2, T3, T4.  \nPotential shapes:  \n- Seven Pairs: currently have 3.5 pairs (W5, W9, B2, plus T2/T3/T4 singles). Need 3.5 more pairs. Unlikely soon.  \n- Standard 4 melds+pair: I have one Chow meld. I need three more melds. Pairs: W5, W9, B2, F3 possible.  \nBest discard should preserve flexibility and tile efficiency.  \n\nPromising discards:  \n1. Play F3: removes a single honor, no strong connections; but F3 is seat wind, keeping could give Seat Wind pattern if I pung it. But only 3 F3 left unseen.  \n2. Play T2, T3, T4: these are consecutive, discarding one breaks a possible Chow sequence. But they are singles with no pairs yet.  \n3. Play W5 or W9: these are pairs; breaking a pair reduces chance for pair for final hand.  \n4. Play B2: a pair, but B2 is simple tile, maybe keep for pair.  \n\nReason:  \nDiscard F3 seems reasonable because:  \n- It\u2019s a single honor with low probability of completing a pung (only 3 left, others may hold).  \n- Keeping T2, T3, T4 allows possible Chow formation with future draws (T1, T4, T5, etc.).  \n- Keeping pairs W5, W9, B2 maintains pair options for final hand.  \n1-point patterns like Seat Wind pung possible, but unlikely given tile count and need for quick melds. Better to aim for hand like Half Flush (only T suit + honors) or All Simples, but my hand has W and B suits too. Not promising for big patterns.  \nSo discard F3 to reduce honor clutter.\n\nAnswer: Play F3"}
